module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.TrivSqZeroExt.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Operations
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalSpace.McQuillanSectionProjections
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# Stage rings of the system of Stacks 0CBE

The morphism of Stacks 0CBE is
`colim_i Spec(Λ/Jᵢ ⊕ Mᵢ) ⟶ Spf(lim_i Λ/Jᵢ)`, built from a system
`Φ = (Λ, Jᵢ ⊂ Λ, (Mᵢ) → (Vᵢ))`: the ideals `Jᵢ` are square-zero and decrease along the index,
the `Mᵢ` form an inverse system of `Λ`-modules with `Mⱼ = Mᵢ/JⱼMᵢ` for `i > j`, and `Mᵢ`
surjects onto the stage `Vᵢ` of a system of vector spaces with vanishing limit. This file
proves what the pairs of stage rings `Rᵢ = Λ/Jᵢ ⊕ Mᵢ` of such a system satisfy, as plain ring
theory and for one pair `i > j` at a time: it is the content that the geometry of Stacks 0CBE
consumes, and it is independent of how a system `Φ` is produced.

Two of the three facts proved here are about a single transition `Rᵢ → Rⱼ`, written
`Stacks.SqZeroExtension.map α p` for `α : Λ/Jᵢ → Λ/Jⱼ` and `p : Mᵢ → Mⱼ` semilinear over it.
It is surjective, and *its kernel is the ideal generated by the image of `ker α`* — that is,
`Rⱼ = Rᵢ ⊗_{Λ/Jᵢ} Λ/Jⱼ`, which is the ring-level form of the representability criterion
`Mⱼ = Mᵢ/JⱼMᵢ` of Stacks 0CBE: the base change of the source along a stage of the target is a
single affine scheme rather than a formal one. The third is that this kernel is square-zero
once `ker α` is, so the transition is a thickening of affine schemes and the colimit of the
`Spec(Rᵢ)` is a candidate affine formal algebraic space.

Both the surjectivity of `Rᵢ → Mᵢ` recorded at the end and the two criteria of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalSpace.McQuillanSectionProjections` are the other half of
the argument: `Rᵢ ↠ Mᵢ ↠ Vᵢ` carries the non-surjectivity of `lim Vᵢ → Vᵢ` to the stage rings
of the source, while `Λ ↠ Λ/Jᵢ` makes the target McQuillan.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.SqZeroExtension.map`: the ring homomorphism `A ⊕ M → B ⊕ N` of trivial square-zero
  extensions attached to a ring homomorphism `α : A →+* B` and an additive map `M → N`
  semilinear over it.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SqZeroExtension.surjective_map`: the transition is surjective when `α` and `p` are.
* `Stacks.SqZeroExtension.ker_map`: **the representability identity.** If `p` has kernel
  `(ker α) • M` then the kernel of the transition is the ideal generated by the image of
  `ker α`; equivalently `B ⊕ N = (A ⊕ M) ⊗_A B`.
* `Stacks.SqZeroExtension.ker_map_mul_self`: that kernel is square-zero as soon as `ker α` is,
  so `Spec(B ⊕ N) ⟶ Spec(A ⊕ M)` is a thickening.
* `Stacks.SqZeroExtension.surjective_snd`: a trivial square-zero extension surjects onto its
  module, which is how a system of modules with non-surjective limit projections obstructs
  McQuillan's criterion for the stage rings built on it.

## Implementation notes

The two modules are *not* taken to be modules over one ring: in Stacks 0CBE the module `Mᵢ` is
a module over `Λ/Jᵢ` and `Mⱼ` over `Λ/Jⱼ`, and the transition is only semilinear over
`Λ/Jᵢ → Λ/Jⱼ`. So `p` is an `AddMonoidHom` together with the semilinearity hypothesis `hp`,
rather than a `LinearMap`; `Λ` itself does not appear, its role being played by the ideal
`ker α` of `A`.

The right action of the opposite ring and its centrality are hypotheses, as they are on
Mathlib's own `TrivSqZeroExt` lemmas: a module over a commutative ring does not carry them
automatically, and supplying them by a global instance would change instance resolution for
every `TrivSqZeroExt` in the library. They are available for free wherever the modules are
concrete.

The kernel hypothesis of `ker_map` is an iff rather than the equality
`LinearMap.ker p = (ker α) • ⊤` because `p` is not linear, so it has no `LinearMap.ker`; the
submodule `(ker α) • ⊤` on the right is formed in `M`, over `A`.

The existence of a system `Φ` is not here. The discussion of Stacks 0CBE produces one by
iterating `Φ ↦ Φ'` from `Φ₀ = (K, (0), (Vᵢ) → (Vᵢ))`, adjoining a variable `x_u` to `Λ` and a
basis vector `ε_{i,u}` to `Mᵢ` for every element `ξ` of a kernel `ker(Mᵢ → Mⱼ)`; a system with
idempotent ideals, and with it the lemma, is built in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalSpace.McQuillanFractionalSystem` and
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalSpace.RepresentableOverMcQuillan`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0CBE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CBE)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.SqZeroExtension

universe u

open TrivSqZeroExt

variable {A B : Type u} [CommRing A] [CommRing B]
  {M : Type u} [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] [Module Aᵐᵒᵖ M] [IsCentralScalar A M]
  {N : Type u} [AddCommGroup N] [Module B N] [Module Bᵐᵒᵖ N] [IsCentralScalar B N]
  (α : A →+* B) (p : M →+ N) (hp : ∀ (a : A) (m : M), p (a • m) = α a • p m)

/-- The ring homomorphism `A ⊕ M → B ⊕ N` of trivial square-zero extensions attached to a ring
homomorphism `α : A →+* B` and an additive map `p : M → N` which is semilinear over it. In
Stacks 0CBE this is the transition morphism `Λ/Jᵢ ⊕ Mᵢ → Λ/Jⱼ ⊕ Mⱼ` between two stage rings of
the source of the morphism. -/
def map : TrivSqZeroExt A M →+* TrivSqZeroExt B N where
  toFun x := ((α x.fst, p x.snd) : TrivSqZeroExt B N)
  map_one' := TrivSqZeroExt.ext (by change α 1 = 1; exact α.map_one)
    (by change p 0 = 0; exact p.map_zero)
  map_zero' := TrivSqZeroExt.ext (by change α 0 = 0; exact α.map_zero)
    (by change p 0 = 0; exact p.map_zero)
  map_add' x y := TrivSqZeroExt.ext
    (by change α (x.fst + y.fst) = α x.fst + α y.fst; exact α.map_add _ _)
    (by change p (x.snd + y.snd) = p x.snd + p y.snd; exact p.map_add _ _)
  map_mul' x y := TrivSqZeroExt.ext
    (by change α (x.fst * y.fst) = α x.fst * α y.fst; exact α.map_mul _ _)
    (by
      change p (x.fst • y.snd + MulOpposite.op y.fst • x.snd)
        = α x.fst • p y.snd + MulOpposite.op (α y.fst) • p x.snd
      rw [op_smul_eq_smul, op_smul_eq_smul, p.map_add, hp, hp])

/-- The ring component of the transition is `α`. -/
@[simp]
theorem fst_map (x : TrivSqZeroExt A M) : (map α p hp x).fst = α x.fst := rfl

/-- The module component of the transition is `p`. -/
@[simp]
theorem snd_map (x : TrivSqZeroExt A M) : (map α p hp x).snd = p x.snd := rfl

/-- An element of `A ⊕ M` is in the kernel of the transition exactly when both of its
components are in the kernels of `α` and of `p`. -/
theorem map_eq_zero_iff {x : TrivSqZeroExt A M} :
    map α p hp x = 0 ↔ α x.fst = 0 ∧ p x.snd = 0 := by
  refine ⟨fun h => ⟨?_, ?_⟩, fun h => TrivSqZeroExt.ext ?_ ?_⟩
  · exact (fst_map α p hp x).symm.trans (congrArg TrivSqZeroExt.fst h)
  · exact (snd_map α p hp x).symm.trans (congrArg TrivSqZeroExt.snd h)
  · exact (fst_map α p hp x).trans (h.1.trans (TrivSqZeroExt.fst_zero).symm)
  · exact (snd_map α p hp x).trans (h.2.trans (TrivSqZeroExt.snd_zero).symm)

/-- The transition of trivial square-zero extensions is surjective as soon as both of its
components are. -/
theorem surjective_map (hα : Function.Surjective α) (hpsurj : Function.Surjective p) :
    Function.Surjective (map α p hp) := fun y => by
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := hα y.fst
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := hpsurj y.snd
  exact ⟨(a, m), TrivSqZeroExt.ext ha hm⟩

/-- The components of an element of the kernel of the transition lie in `ker α` and in
`(ker α) • M` respectively. -/
private theorem mem_ker_of_mem_ker_map
    (hker : ∀ m : M, p m = 0 ↔ m ∈ (RingHom.ker α) • (⊤ : Submodule A M))
    {x : TrivSqZeroExt A M} (hx : x ∈ RingHom.ker (map α p hp)) :
    x.fst ∈ RingHom.ker α ∧ x.snd ∈ (RingHom.ker α) • (⊤ : Submodule A M) := by
  obtain ⟨hfst, hsnd⟩ := (map_eq_zero_iff α p hp).mp (RingHom.mem_ker.mp hx)
  exact ⟨RingHom.mem_ker.mpr hfst, (hker x.snd).mp hsnd⟩

/-- **The representability identity of Stacks 0CBE.** If the kernel of `p` is the submodule
`(ker α) • M`, then the kernel of the transition `A ⊕ M → B ⊕ N` is the ideal generated by the
image of `ker α` — that is, `B ⊕ N` is the base change `(A ⊕ M) ⊗_A B`. In Stacks 0CBE this is
the condition `Mⱼ = Mᵢ/JⱼMᵢ`, which makes the base change of the source of the morphism along a
stage of its target the single affine scheme `Spec(Λ/Jⱼ ⊕ Mⱼ)`. -/
theorem ker_map (hker : ∀ m : M, p m = 0 ↔ m ∈ (RingHom.ker α) • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) :
    RingHom.ker (map α p hp) =
      Ideal.map (inlHom A M) (RingHom.ker α) := by
  apply le_antisymm
  · intro x hx
    obtain ⟨hfst, hsnd'⟩ := mem_ker_of_mem_ker_map α p hp hker hx
    have hinl : (inl x.fst : TrivSqZeroExt A M) ∈
        Ideal.map (inlHom A M) (RingHom.ker α) :=
      Ideal.mem_map_of_mem _ hfst
    have hinr : (inr x.snd : TrivSqZeroExt A M) ∈
        Ideal.map (inlHom A M) (RingHom.ker α) := by
      refine Submodule.smul_induction_on hsnd' (fun a ha m _ => ?_) (fun y z hy hz => ?_)
      · rw [← inl_mul_inr]
        exact Ideal.mul_mem_right _ _ (Ideal.mem_map_of_mem _ ha)
      · rw [inr_add]
        exact Ideal.add_mem _ hy hz
    have hsum := Ideal.add_mem _ hinl hinr
    rwa [inl_fst_add_inr_snd_eq] at hsum
  · rw [Ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap]
    intro a ha
    refine RingHom.mem_ker.mpr ((map_eq_zero_iff α p hp).mpr ⟨?_, ?_⟩)
    · exact RingHom.mem_ker.mp ha
    · change p 0 = 0
      exact p.map_zero

/-- The kernel of a transition of trivial square-zero extensions is square-zero as soon as the
kernel of its ring component is. With `ker_map` and `surjective_map` this makes
`Spec(B ⊕ N) ⟶ Spec(A ⊕ M)` a thickening of affine schemes, which is what a presentation of an
affine formal algebraic space asks of its transition morphisms. -/
theorem ker_map_mul_self (hker : ∀ m : M, p m = 0 ↔ m ∈ (RingHom.ker α) • (⊤ : Submodule A M))
    (hsq : RingHom.ker α * RingHom.ker α = ⊥) :
    RingHom.ker (map α p hp) * RingHom.ker (map α p hp) = ⊥ := by
  have hsmul : (RingHom.ker α) • ((RingHom.ker α) • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) = ⊥ := by
    rw [← Submodule.smul_assoc, Ideal.smul_eq_mul, hsq, Submodule.bot_smul]
  have hzero : ∀ ⦃a : A⦄, a ∈ RingHom.ker α → ∀ ⦃m : M⦄,
      m ∈ (RingHom.ker α) • (⊤ : Submodule A M) → a • m = 0 := by
    intro a ha m hm
    have hmem : a • m ∈ (RingHom.ker α) • ((RingHom.ker α) • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) :=
      Submodule.smul_mem_smul ha hm
    rwa [hsmul, Submodule.mem_bot] at hmem
  refine le_antisymm (Ideal.mul_le.mpr fun x hx y hy => ?_) bot_le
  obtain ⟨hxf', hxs'⟩ := mem_ker_of_mem_ker_map α p hp hker hx
  obtain ⟨hyf', hys'⟩ := mem_ker_of_mem_ker_map α p hp hker hy
  rw [Submodule.mem_bot]
  refine TrivSqZeroExt.ext ?_ ?_
  · have hprod : x.fst * y.fst ∈ RingHom.ker α * RingHom.ker α :=
      Ideal.mul_mem_mul hxf' hyf'
    rw [hsq, Submodule.mem_bot] at hprod
    change x.fst * y.fst = (0 : TrivSqZeroExt A M).fst
    rw [hprod, TrivSqZeroExt.fst_zero]
  · change x.fst • y.snd + MulOpposite.op y.fst • x.snd = (0 : TrivSqZeroExt A M).snd
    rw [op_smul_eq_smul, hzero hxf' hys', hzero hyf' hxs', add_zero, TrivSqZeroExt.snd_zero]

omit [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] [Module Aᵐᵒᵖ M] [IsCentralScalar A M] in
/-- A trivial square-zero extension surjects onto its module. Composed with the surjections
`Mᵢ ↠ Vᵢ` of Stacks 0CBE this is the componentwise surjection that carries the
non-surjectivity of `lim Vᵢ → Vᵢ` to the stage rings of the source, by
`CategoryTheory.Functor.not_surjective_sections_eval_of_natTrans`. -/
theorem surjective_snd : Function.Surjective (TrivSqZeroExt.snd : TrivSqZeroExt A M → M) :=
  fun m => ⟨inr m, rfl⟩

omit [CommRing A] [Module A M] [Module Aᵐᵒᵖ M] [IsCentralScalar A M] in
/-- A trivial square-zero extension surjects onto its base ring. -/
@[ega "0 18.2.3" "surjectivity of f"]
theorem surjective_fst : Function.Surjective (TrivSqZeroExt.fst : TrivSqZeroExt A M → A) :=
  fun a => ⟨inl a, rfl⟩

end Stacks.SqZeroExtension

end
